---
import Layout from '../layouts/StandardLayout.astro';
const example = `{
  "id": "agdr-12",
  "related": [
    { "rel": "implements", "type": "orbit.execution-slice", "id": "slice-004" },
    { "rel": "satisfies", "type": "orbit.acceptance-criterion", "id": "ac-2" }
  ],
  "extensions": {}
}`;
const flow = `ORBIT Plan
    ↓
Reconciliation
    ↓
Execution Slice
    ↓
Agent
 ├──→ Code
 └──→ AgDR`;
---
<Layout title="Interoperability"><section class="page wide"><p class="pathbar">~/agent-sdlc / interoperability</p><p class="eyebrow">Agent SDLC Interoperability Profile · v0.1</p><h1>How they work together.</h1><p class="lede">ORBIT determines what change is justified next. AgDR preserves why a consequential technical decision was made during that work.</p><p>Neither standard depends on the other. Each remains independently conformant. The profile defines a small, optional relationship vocabulary for artefacts that need to reference one another.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Informative profile.</strong> These relationships are examples until the relevant standards adopt them normatively.</div><div class="split"><div><p class="section-label">RELATIONSHIP</p><h2>Independent standards.<br />Composable artefacts.</h2><p>Stable identifiers and typed relationships connect records without creating a shared runtime or universal schema.</p></div><pre class="code">{flow}</pre></div><div class="split"><div><p class="section-label">EXAMPLE</p><h2>An AgDR can point to the work it explains.</h2><p>This example links a decision record to an ORBIT execution slice and acceptance criterion. The link is optional.</p></div><pre class="code">{example}</pre></div><h2>Relationship shape</h2><p>Each relationship has a <code>rel</code>, a registered or URI-like <code>type</code>, and a target <code>id</code>. An optional <code>href</code> supplies a portable URL. Targets may be unresolved. Relationship order has no meaning. Producers should avoid duplicate relationship objects.</p><h2>Initial vocabulary</h2><div class="vocabulary"><code>related-to</code><code>implements</code><code>contributes-to</code><code>satisfies</code><code>derived-from</code><code>supersedes</code><code>decision-record</code><code>deliberation-record</code></div><h2>Extension boundary</h2><p>Use <code>extensions</code> for producer-specific fields. Namespaces keep those fields separate from the core artefact contract. ORBIT does not require AgDR, and AgDR does not require ORBIT.</p></section></Layout>
